# Aligning Table Captions Left in LaTeX

Without the `caption` package, you can use these approaches:

## 1. **Using `\raggedright` (Simplest)**

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
  {\raggedright
    \caption{This is my table caption}
    \label{tab:example}
  }
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
    \hline
    A & B \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}
```

## 2. **Using a `minipage`**

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
  \begin{minipage}{\textwidth}
    \raggedright
    \caption{This is my table caption}
    \label{tab:example}
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
      \hline
      A & B \\
      \hline
    \end{tabular}
  \end{minipage}
\end{table}
```

## 3. **Using a `parbox`**

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
  \parbox{\textwidth}{%
    \raggedright
    \caption{This is my table caption}
    \label{tab:example}
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
      \hline
      A & B \\
      \hline
    \end{tabular}
  }
\end{table}
```

## Key Points:
- `\raggedright` left-aligns the caption
- Use `\centering` *after* the caption if you want the table itself centered
- The order matters: caption first, then table
- Method 1 is the most straightforward for simple cases

Choose **Method 1** for most situations—it's the cleanest and most commonly used approach.

